<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mauretania Sitifensis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mauretania Sitifensis was a Roman province in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, created under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> around 300 CE by dividing off the eastern part of Mauretania Caesariensis. Its capital was Sitifis, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setif">Sétif</a>, in a rich grain-growing upland. It was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> in the fifth century, recovered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in the sixth and finally absorbed in the Arab conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mauretania-sitifensis.jpg" alt="Mauretania Sitifensis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas; derivative work by Jbribeiro1 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Spain_-_AD_400.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
